    excerpt:
      "From lemon peels in the disposal to chemical drain cleaners being safe — these plumbing myths cost homeowners real money. Here is what actually works.",
    content: `
      <p>When it comes to home plumbing, a mix of passed-down advice, internet life hacks, and old wives’ tales circulates widely — and a lot of it is wrong. For homeowners in Temecula, Murrieta, Menifee, Lake Elsinore, and Wildomar, acting on bad plumbing advice can cause damage that far exceeds what the original problem would have cost to fix. Separating fact from fiction is the fastest way to protect your pipes and your wallet.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-red-400 mb-4">Myth 1: Lemons Clean the Garbage Disposal</h2>
      <p>Tossing citrus peels into the disposal can mask odors briefly, but it does not truly clean the unit. Thick rinds can be hard on the blades and may contribute to clogs. Safer cleaning methods: grind a handful of ice cubes to knock off food residue, scrub the rubber splash guard with a small brush and dish soap (power off first), and always run cold water for 20–30 seconds after using the disposal to push material through the trap. If odors or buildup persist, a professional inspection can assess whether components need cleaning, replacement parts, or whether an older, noisy unit is due for a full replacement. Gardner Plumbing Co. handles <a href="/services/plumbing/garbage-disposal-service" class="text-blue-400 hover:text-blue-300 underline">garbage disposal service</a> with upfront flat-rate options.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-red-400 mt-10 mb-4">Myth 2: If It Goes Down, There Is No Clog</h2>
      <p>A drain that empties — just slowly — is often the first sign of a developing blockage. Hair, soap scum, and grease cling to pipe walls and gradually restrict flow long before a full clog forms. Homeowners can try removing and cleaning the stopper, using a plunger, or running a hand auger. If those steps fail, options include enzymatic drain treatments, professional snaking, <a href="/services/plumbing/hydro-jetting" class="text-blue-400 hover:text-blue-300 underline">hydro-jetting</a>, or a camera inspection to see what is actually in the line. In older neighborhoods around Murrieta, Hemet, or Lake Elsinore, tree roots may be invading sewer lines and require targeted repair — a camera inspection is the only way to confirm it. For city-specific drain service, see our <a href="/services/plumbing/murrieta-drain-cleaning" class="text-blue-400 hover:text-blue-300 underline">Murrieta drain cleaning</a> and <a href="/services/plumbing/hemet-drain-cleaning" class="text-blue-400 hover:text-blue-300 underline">Hemet drain cleaning</a> pages.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-red-400 mt-10 mb-4">Myth 3: Chemical Drain Cleaners Are Safe and Effective</h2>
      <p>Store-bought liquid drain cleaners use caustic reactions and heat to dissolve blockages. They can clear minor clogs occasionally, but repeated use may soften PVC piping, accelerate corrosion in older metal pipes, and harm septic systems. Mixing different drain chemicals creates toxic fumes — a genuine safety hazard. The better approach for stubborn clogs is a mechanical method (plunging, snaking), an enzyme-based cleaner for organic buildup, or professional hydro-jetting for heavy scale and grease accumulation. Camera-guided diagnosis ensures the right tool is used for the actual problem rather than guessing.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-red-400 mt-10 mb-4">Myth 4: "Flushable" Wipes Are Safe for the Toilet</h2>
      <p>This myth costs homeowners across Riverside County significant money every year. Wipes labeled "flushable" are made of sturdy, non-dispersing fibers that do not break down like toilet paper. They catch on imperfections in sewer lines and accumulate other debris until they form large blockages — often deep in the line where snaking alone cannot clear them. The only things safe to flush are human waste and standard toilet paper. When a sewer backup occurs, especially if multiple fixtures back up simultaneously, call a licensed plumber immediately. Sewage backup is a health hazard and can cause serious property damage. Gardner Plumbing handles <a href="/services/plumbing/backups" class="text-blue-400 hover:text-blue-300 underline">plumbing backups</a> with same-day urgency throughout Riverside County.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-red-400 mt-10 mb-4">Myth 5: A Leaky Faucet Is Just an Annoyance</h2>
      <p>A faucet that drips once per second wastes over 3,000 gallons in a year. In Riverside County, where tiered water rates apply and conservation is a community priority, a persistent drip is a financial and environmental problem. Beyond the water cost, a slow drip under a sink can quietly rot cabinet floors, warp countertops, and fuel mold growth — all problems discovered when the damage is already done. Early repair — whether a cartridge swap or a full fixture upgrade — is far cheaper than addressing the secondary damage. See our guide on <a href="/blog/repair-vs-replace-leaky-faucet" class="text-blue-400 hover:text-blue-300 underline">when to repair vs. replace a leaky faucet</a> for a full breakdown of the options.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-red-400 mt-10 mb-4">Myth 6: In-Tank Toilet Cleaners Are Good Maintenance</h2>
      <p>Drop-in bleach tablets and blue tank disks may keep the bowl looking clean, but concentrated chlorine chemicals degrade the rubber and plastic components inside the tank — flappers, fill valves, seals — causing running toilets and premature failures. A running toilet can waste hundreds of gallons per day. Safer alternatives include manual bowl cleaning, white vinegar for mineral buildup (very relevant in high-mineral areas like Menifee and Perris), and scheduled part inspections. If a tablet has already caused a running toilet, a Gardner technician can assess which components need replacement. See our <a href="/services/plumbing/toilet-installation-and-repair" class="text-blue-400 hover:text-blue-300 underline">toilet repair service</a> page for details.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-red-400 mt-10 mb-4">Myth 7: Garbage Disposals Can Handle Any Food Waste</h2>
      <p>Disposals are designed for small, soft food scraps — not all food waste. Grease and oil cool and coat pipe walls, starches like rice and pasta swell into pasty clogs, and fibrous items like celery and artichokes can tangle the impellers. The safest habit is to use the disposal sparingly, run cold water before and after, and send the majority of solid food waste to the trash or compost. If a disposal jams, turn off the power before troubleshooting and follow the manufacturer’s reset procedure. Persistent problems should prompt a professional inspection before a DIY attempt causes additional damage.</p>

      <p class="mt-6 p-4 border border-red-400 rounded text-white">Have a plumbing question or a problem that internet advice made worse? Call <a href="tel:9512464337" class="text-blue-400 hover:text-blue-300 underline">(951) 246-4337</a> — no trip fees, no obligation. Gardner Plumbing Co. (CSLB #1073177) serves Temecula, Murrieta, Menifee, Lake Elsinore, Perris, and 20+ additional cities across Riverside County.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-red-400 mt-10 mb-4">Accurate Information Protects Your Plumbing</h2>
      <p>The myths covered above share one thing in common: they all make a problem seem smaller than it is or make a bad solution seem harmless. When in doubt about a plumbing practice, consulting a licensed professional is always the right call. Gardner Plumbing Co. has served Riverside County for 30+ years, provides flat-rate pricing on all services, and backs every job with a 30-day callback guarantee.</p>
    `,
